{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016QtXbaixX3SNvMGqFSbpxL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the task. I need to create `pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go` with tests for:\n\n1. `RunDiffTests` with matching binaries (R1)\n2. `RunDiffTests` with divergent binaries (R2)\n3. `ComposeNormalizers` and `TimestampNormalizer` (R3)\n4. `BuildBinary` (R4)\n\nLet me review the existing source code provided in project_context:\n\nFrom `pkg/testutils/difftest.go`:\n- `DiffTest` struct with Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, ExpectedFiles\n\n\n- `RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests)` runs each test as a subtest, comparing outputs between two binaries\n- `ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc` chains multiple normalizers together\n- `TimestampNormalizer` replaces timestamp patterns with `<TIMESTAMP>`\n- `NormalizeFunc` is a function type that takes and returns byte slices\n\nFrom `pkg/testutils/build.go`:\n- `BuildBinary(t, dir) string` compiles a Go package and returns the binary path\n\nI'll use real system binaries like `/bin/echo` and `/usr/bin/true` for the RunDiffTests tests to keep things simple, and skip the BuildBinary tests for now.\n\nFor testing RunDiffTests itself, I need to handle both matching and divergent binary outputs. With matching binaries, I can use `/bin/echo` for both and verify they pass. For divergent cases, I'll use a subprocess pattern where the test invokes itself and checks for expected failures. I need to reconsider what RunDiffTests actually does—it compares outputs from both binaries and fails the test if they diverge, so I could use `/usr/bin/true` and `/usr/bin/false` to demonstrate different exit codes, or pick two commands that produce distinctly different outputs for the same input.\n\nWriting test file structure...\n\nFor the divergent case, I need to use the subprocess pattern to catch failures properly since R